<section style="padding:72px 24px 40px;"><div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;"><div class="rich"><p>Kings Park Structural Co puts home owners in the Western Suburbs &amp; Fremantle in touch with an independent structural engineering firm, from Cottesloe, Claremont, Nedlands and Mosman Park down to Fremantle and East Fremantle. We're a referral service, and the firm we refer to pays to be listed here. It quotes you and contracts with you directly. We don't inspect, design or certify anything ourselves.</p><p>WA builds in brick more than any other state: a 2025 HIA-commissioned report put it at 68 per cent of detached houses. In double brick, the inner leaf of the external walls and some internal walls usually hold up the ceilings and roof, which is why a kitchen wall is rarely just a kitchen wall. Add salt air, Perth's coastal strip of Tamala Limestone and Fremantle's heritage areas, and there are a lot of questions worth putting to an engineer before anyone picks up a sledgehammer.</p></div></div></section>
<section style="padding:0 24px 64px;"><div style="max-width:1100px;margin:0 auto;">
    <span class="kicker">What We Do in Western Suburbs & Fremantle</span>
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 28px;">Services for Western Suburbs & Fremantle Properties</h2>
    <div class="feat-grid" style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr);gap:20px;">
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #ECE5D8;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Figtree',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#15191E;margin:0 0 8px;">Wall removal and beam design</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">Opening up a kitchen in a double-brick house in Nedlands or Claremont? The engineer works out whether the wall is load-bearing and specifies the beam, posts and footings the building permit needs.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #ECE5D8;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Figtree',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#15191E;margin:0 0 8px;">Crack and lintel inspections</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">Step cracks in brickwork, or cracks running from the corners of windows and doors near the coast. An engineer's report records what's there, what's likely causing it and what should happen next.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #ECE5D8;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Figtree',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#15191E;margin:0 0 8px;">Second storeys and extensions</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">Adding a level or an extension changes floor area or height, so it needs a building permit and engineering. The engineer who quotes your job checks whether the existing walls and footings can take the extra load.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #ECE5D8;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Figtree',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#15191E;margin:0 0 8px;">Patio, carport and shed certificates</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">A roofed alfresco in Mosman Park or a carport in Fremantle isn't an exempt pergola, so it usually goes through a building permit. The engineer's certificate (often called a certificate of structural adequacy) goes in with the application.</p>
      </div>
</div>
